[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me make sure I have the setup right: net margin fell from 24% to 6% — that's a loss of 18 points of margin — while revenue grew 15% over two years. So profit dollars have actually fallen sharply. If revenue was 100 before, profit was 24; now revenue is 115 and profit is 6.9. That's roughly a 71% decline in absolute profit. Big problem.

Since revenue is up, this looks cost-driven, but I want to confirm revenue quality before I assume that.

My approach has three buckets. First, decompose revenue growth: is the 15% from new store openings, same-store traffic, or price? New stores could be dilutive if they're underperforming or carrying ramp-up costs. Second, walk the cost structure — COGS (beans, dairy, cups), labor, occupancy/rent, and corporate overhead — and compare each as a percent of revenue two years ago versus today to find where the 18 points went. Third, look externally: commodity prices, wage inflation, competition.

Can I start by asking how the 15% revenue growth splits between new stores and same-store sales?
