[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:35] I don't have the split between new stores and same-store sales. Looking at your three buckets, is that structure MECE, and what might be missing?

[candidate · 3:05] Fair challenge. Revenue and cost are MECE for profit, so the spine is fine. But my third bucket — external factors — isn't a separate branch; commodity prices and wages are drivers *of* the cost lines, so I'd fold them underneath rather than list them in parallel. That was sloppy.

Two things I'd add. One, I collapsed everything below gross profit into "overhead," but net margin includes items that aren't operating costs — interest expense if they've levered up to fund expansion, depreciation on new store buildouts, taxes, one-time charges like impairments or lease exits. An 18-point swing in *net* margin could partly sit there, not in operations. Two, a mix branch: within revenue, shifts between food and beverage, or retail bags versus in-store drinks, change blended margin even if no single cost line moves.

So revised: profit = revenue (volume, price, mix) minus operating costs (COGS, labor, occupancy, corporate) minus non-operating items. Which of those do we have data on?
