[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 6:19] The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit. Understood. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse this decline? Think broadly.

[candidate · 7:49] Quick reconciliation: revenue +15% with ticket +10% implies transaction count up only 1.15/1.10 = 4.5%. So traffic is nearly flat and most of the growth is basket size. That's consistent with prices being flat — but it refines my earlier statement: the volume growth is items per visit, not more customers. It also means COGS scaling with the 15% item volume checks out.

Beyond pricing, which is the obvious first lever given two years of flat menu prices against 40% inflation:

Procurement — hedge or forward-contract beans, renegotiate dairy and packaging contracts, consolidate suppliers for scale. At $208M, a 5% saving on other inputs is $10M, two points of margin.

Mix and portion — push higher-margin items, right-size portions, reduce waste and spoilage.

Product reformulation — cheaper bean blends risk brand damage, but packaging is invisible to customers.

Revenue per store — traffic is flat at 200 stores; dayparts, loyalty, and food attach could lift it.

Can I ask what competitors have done on price over the same period?
