[interviewer · WRAP · 9:25] Understood. Pull this together for the CEO: what's your recommendation, including what you'd do first and what risks you'd flag?

[candidate · 10:55] Recommendation: Brew & Bean's profit decline is almost entirely an input-cost pass-through failure, and the fix is a staged price increase of roughly 10–15% paired with procurement savings.

The evidence: COGS rose from 42% to 58% of revenue, explaining 16 of the 18 margin points. Labor held flat at 22% and overhead moved only two points, so this isn't an operating-efficiency problem. Input costs rose ~40% while menu prices haven't moved in two years — the numbers tie out exactly, so there's no hidden fourth factor.

What I'd do first: a 60-day price test in 15–20 stores at two price points, measuring transaction counts, and in parallel a competitive price audit, since we don't have peer pricing. Simultaneously open renegotiation on the non-bean inputs — that's 75% of COGS and $208M, so 5% there is two margin points with no customer impact.

Risks: elasticity is unknown and traffic is already nearly flat at ~4.5% growth, so we may be more fragile than the headline 15% suggests. A price move could expose that. Second, bean markets could keep rising — I'd hedge. Third, brand risk if we cut quality instead of raising price.

Done well, 10% price plus procurement gets them to roughly 12% margin within a year.
